Z||000012||Formula88||02-11-2001||09:29 PM||||Still looks like Fiero Rice to me.  Dump the ridiculously huge wheels and that spoiler.  Thank god it only exists on computer like that....||||reg||9
Z||000013||TwisT||02-11-2001||10:03 PM||||LOL Formula...<P>I think what does the "riceing" is the rocker pannels combined with the air intake.  Looks too pieced together.  My GT will most likely keep the factory rockers and use just the air intake.<P>I don't think it's fair to call the wing "ricey,"  as it is after all from an American-made Mustang...  and...  looks NOTHING like those rediculous contraptions the ricer's use.  Not even close.  Again,  the wing actually matches and "flows" with the Fiero's lines quite nicely.<P>The wheels are NOT ricey either,  strictly Italian;  think Diablo.  In fact,  the Antera wheels are made in Europe,  NOT Japan.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Silver multi-piece wheels with polished lips SCREAM Italian.  You don't have to own a ricer to own nice wheels.<P>I don't think a computer-enhanced picture of a Fiero can do any justice to the real thing.  Keep your mind open to what it is,  a pallet for ideas.<P>Brett  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Shaft||02-11-2001||10:11 PM||||That's not rice. There are no decals all over the car or fake badging. I think that is a very clean looking fiero, with the molding removed, lotus-type side skirts, lower peices on the rear facia, front spoiler, rear spoiler and cool side scoops. The rims are big though, but it looks cooler with big ones. I'd never go smaller that 17"s on a fiero. I just wish we could make a car that really looked like that.||||reg||10
Z||000015||TwisT||02-11-2001||10:44 PM||||Shaft...  but you can!<P>These pictures that I've done on the computer are all "imagination verifications,"  in other words,  pictures showing REALISTIC modifications.  Ones that you or I could do without spending thousands or learning new trades.  There isn't anything in these pictures that you couldn't do,  if not easily.<P>I always get new design ideas for the Fiero.  The first thing I do is go to my hard drive and look for a suitable photo that I can use to create a certain idea.  I keep a collection of this stuff so that when I'm ready to actually follow through on the Fiero itself,  I have everything in front of me.  Not all ideas work out.  Some end up not looking good at all.  Others simply drop my jaw.<P>My ideas for my "ItalFiero" project have always been kept simple.  I'm going for a clean "Italian" Ferrari-like approach.  No crazy "extended wing pedestals" or gaudy scoops or racing stripes.  No mega-buck mods.  Only clean,  flowing lines and subtle enhancements.<P>I do have a website about this car.  However,  it's currently under HEAVY reconstruction.  When it's ready,  everyone can watch what's really going down.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Brett  ||||reg||1
